# deployer

Builds projects in parallel, packs each one, ships it to its target over SSH,
and runs a handoff hook. Live terminal interface; plain output for CI.

It is deliberately stack-agnostic. A project is *a command that produces a
directory* plus *somewhere to send it*, so .NET, Angular and a folder someone
precompiled on a Windows box three weeks ago are all the same shape.

## What it does not do

It stops once the archive has landed and the `post_ship` hook has run.
Unpacking it, swapping the live release, restarting services, health checks —
that belongs to whatever runs on the server, not to the thing that delivered
the file. Keeping that line sharp is why the tool stays small.

## Architecture

```
  deploy.toml ──► config ──► steps.Pipeline ──┐
                                               │
                              ┌────────────────▼─────────────────┐
                              │  engine.Runner                   │
                              │  N pipelines in parallel,        │
                              │  steps sequential within one     │
                              └────────────────┬─────────────────┘
                                               │ chan engine.Event
                           ┌───────────────────┴──────────────────┐
                           ▼                                      ▼
                    ui/tui (Bubble Tea)                     ui/plain (CI)
```

The engine knows nothing about builds, archives or SSH — it runs `Step`s and
emits events. Both renderers are pure consumers of that one stream, so neither
is load-bearing and the engine stays testable without a terminal.

| Package | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `internal/config` | Parse and validate the plan. A config that survives `Load` is runnable. |
| `internal/engine` | Event types, the `Step` interface, the parallel runner. |
| `internal/steps` | `build` → `pack` → `ship` → `handoff`. The only package that knows what a deploy is. |
| `internal/transport` | SSH connection, plus `sftp` and `scp` behind one interface. |
| `internal/ui/*` | Rendering. No logic. |

### Why SFTP is the default

Both transports speak to the same sshd with the same credentials. SFTP runs
over the connection we already hold, so we own the byte stream and progress is
exact rather than scraped out of another program's output — and it never needs
a shell on the far side, which matters on Windows where the default shell is
anybody's guess. `scp` is kept as a genuine fallback: it shares none of that
code path, so when something is wrong with ours it reaches the server the same
way you would by hand.

## Build

```sh
cd ~/docs/office/deployer
go mod tidy          # resolve dependencies, write go.sum
go build -o deploy ./cmd/deploy
```

## Use

```sh
cp deploy.example.toml deploy.toml   # then fill in the targets
./deploy --list                      # show the plan
./deploy --dry-run                   # walk it without touching anything
./deploy                             # interactive
./deploy --only hrms-api,hrms-web    # a subset
./deploy --plain --verbose           # for CI, or a connection too thin for a TUI
```

Keys: `↑↓` pick a project · `a` follow all logs · `g`/`G` top/bottom · `q`
cancel the run, again to quit.

A clean run closes itself. A failed one stays up, because that log is why you
ran it.

## Config

```toml
jobs = 3                       # parallel projects; default NumCPU/2

[targets.prod-linux]
host = "1.2.3.4"
port = 22                      # default 22
user = "deploy"
key  = "~/.ssh/id_ed25519"     # omit to use the SSH agent
shell = "sh"                   # sh | powershell | cmd
insecure_skip_host_key_check = false

[[projects]]
name      = "api"
dir       = "../api"           # build working dir, relative to this file
build     = "dotnet publish -c Release -o {out}"
artifact  = "{out}"            # what gets packed; default "{out}"
env       = { ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT = "Production" }
exclude   = ["wwwroot/Uploads", "*.pdb"]
archive   = "tar.zst"          # tar.zst | tar.gz
transport = "sftp"             # sftp | scp
target    = "prod-linux"
remote    = "/srv/artifacts/api"
post_ship = "docker compose -f /srv/api/compose.yml up -d"
enabled   = true
```

Placeholders: `{out}` and `{name}` in `build`/`artifact`; `{archive}`, `{dir}`
and `{name}` in `post_ship`.

Omit `build` to ship a directory that already exists — set `artifact` to it.

### exclude

Patterns match the path relative to the artifact root, three ways:

- an exact path or anything under it — `wwwroot/OfferLetters`
- a glob on the whole relative path — `logs/*.txt`
- a glob on the file name alone — `*.pdb`

**This is load-bearing.** `SolutionOneWebPortal/wwwroot` holds uploaded offer
letters, joining letters, leave attachments and profile images — live user
data that is not in the build output. If the server-side step extracts over
the site root, anything not excluded here is anything that gets destroyed.
The example plan excludes them; keep it that way.

## Testing

```sh
docker compose -f test/compose.yml up -d
./deploy --config test/deploy.test.toml
docker compose -f test/compose.yml down -v      # reset
```

A free, disposable SSH target that exercises the real connection, both
transports, the `.part` rename and the handoff hook. The test plan includes a
~120 MB project so progress reporting and concurrent writes get a real
workout, and a deliberately broken one (`--only broken`) to confirm a failure
is contained to its own pipeline.

It is Linux, so it does **not** cover the legacy Windows path. Before trusting
a deploy to that box, run one against a Windows Server evaluation VM: IIS file
locking, `tar.exe`'s zstd support and PowerShell-over-SSH quoting are all
things only a real Windows target will tell you the truth about.

## Known rough edges

- `scp` reports no progress until the file lands. OpenSSH's progress output is
  not stable across versions and parsing it would be worse than not having it.
  Use `sftp` if you want a bar.
- `tar.zst` needs a zstd-aware tar on the far side. Modern Linux is fine;
  older Windows Server builds are not, hence `archive = "tar.gz"` per project.
- Passphrase-protected keys are not prompted for. Add them to your agent;
  the error says so.

## Likely next

- Content-hash skip — don't rebuild or re-upload an artifact that hasn't changed.
- A `registry` transport: build an image and push it, for projects whose
  server side is compose anyway. The `Transport` interface already allows it.
- `rsync` transport for Linux targets, once the frontend's 201 MB is worth
  delta-transferring.
